---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import HtmlEscapeTool from '../../components/HtmlEscapeTool.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="HTML Escape / Unescape Tool" description="Escape special characters before rendering user input as HTML, or unescape HTML entities back to plain text.">
  <HtmlEscapeTool client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why escaping is a core XSS defense</h2>
  <p>If user-submitted text is inserted directly into HTML without escaping, a malicious user can embed a script tag that executes in other users' browsers — one of the most common vulnerability classes on the web. Escaping the six special characters neutralizes this by ensuring the input is treated as text, not markup.</p>

  <h2>How to use this tool</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose "Escape" to convert special characters to safe entities, or "Unescape" to reverse it.</li>
    <li>Paste your text and copy the result.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Escaping <code>&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;</code> produces <code>&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;alert(1)&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;</code> — safe to render as literal text without executing as markup.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Escaping on output only sometimes</strong>, missing a code path where user input reaches the DOM unescaped.</li>
    <li><strong>Relying on manual escaping instead of your framework's built-in protections</strong> — most modern frontend frameworks auto-escape by default; manual escaping is mainly needed when deliberately bypassing that (like using <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Double-escaping already-escaped content</strong>, producing visible entity codes instead of the intended characters.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Safely displaying user-submitted text that might contain HTML-special characters</li>
    <li>Debugging why entity codes are appearing literally instead of rendering as characters</li>
    <li>Understanding what a framework's auto-escaping is actually doing under the hood</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does escaping alone fully prevent XSS?</strong> It's the core defense for text content, but XSS prevention also depends on context — escaping for HTML attributes, URLs, and JavaScript contexts each have different rules.</p>
</ToolLayout>
